NiceMon V9.9, BASIC V9.9
HELP lists the statements
READY.
help
SYS A               call the program at A, such as SYS 0x8000; SYS 0 restarts
LOAD                enter lines of ADDR WORD in hex, as a .bin file has them
LOAD "NAME" [, S]   load the program NAME.BAS, or NAME.BIN, from the SD card
LOAD "NAME", "IP"   load it from the TFTP server at IP
SAVE "NAME" [, S]   save the program as the text NAME.BAS
BSAVE "NAME", A, N [, S]  save N words from A as the .bin file NAME
SAVE "NAME", "IP"   save it to the TFTP server at IP (nicemon.py tftpd --write)
BSAVE "NAME", A, N, "IP"  save the words to the TFTP server at IP
DELETE "NAME" [, S] delete the file NAME of the SD card
DELETE N [- M]      delete the lines from N to M
DIR [S]             list the files of the card in slot S, or of the one mounted
DIR "PATH"          list the files of the folder PATH, such as "GAMES/OLD"
DIR "IP" [, "PAT"]  list the files of nicemon.py tftpd at IP, or those of PAT
  S, a slot, mounts its card first, for the statements after it too
DUMP [A] [, N]      show N words from A, in hex, or the next ones
DIS [A] [, N]       disassemble N instructions from A, or the next ones
IP ["A.B.C.D"]      show the IP address, or set it, such as "192.168.1.65"
DHCP                get the IP address from a DHCP server
MAC ["MAC"]         show the MAC address, or set it, "02:4E:49:43:45:01"
BAUD [RATE]         show the UART baud rate, or set it, such as 921600
TIME ["D T"]        show the local time, or set it, "2026-11-11 14:30:00"
NTP ["S"] [, "Z"]   set it from time server S, Z local time less UTC, "+01:00"
CLS                 clear the screen
VER                 the version
HALT                halt the core
HELP                the statements
PRINT X; "T", ...   print numbers and strings, ; and , between them; ? is PRINT
LET V = X           set the variable V, or V$ to a string; LET may be left out
POKE A, X           write X to the word at A, which PEEK(A) reads
RUN [N]             run the program, from line N or its first, after CLR
RUN "NAME" [, S]    load NAME and run it; NAME.BIN at 0x8000
RUN "NAME", "IP"    load NAME from the TFTP server at IP and run it
LIST [N] [- M]      list the lines of the program, those from N to M
NEW                 delete the program and its variables
CLR                 clear the variables
GOTO N              go on at line N
GOSUB N             go on at line N, and back after it at RETURN
RETURN              go back after the last GOSUB
IF X THEN S ELSE T  S when X is not 0, else T; each statements or a line N
FOR V = A TO B      V from A to B, by 1 or by STEP C after B, up to NEXT
NEXT [V]            the loop of FOR again, or its end
END                 end the program
STOP                stop the program, which CONT goes on with
CONT                go on after STOP, END, or the break key
REM TEXT            a remark, the rest of the line
INPUT ["P";] V, ... the items of a line typed into V, ..., after the prompt P
GET V               V the code of the next key typed, 0 when none; V$ the key
DIM A(N), B$(N, M)  the arrays A of N + 1 elements, B$ of N + 1 rows of M + 1
HIMEM = A           the top of the program, its variables, and arrays; and CLR
AUTO [N] [, S]      number the lines typed from N, by S; a number alone ends it
RENUMBER [N] [, S]  number the lines from N, by S, and the jumps with them
DATA 1, "A,B", C    the items that READ reads, numbers and strings
READ V, W$, ...     V and W$ the next items of DATA
RESTORE [N]         READ from the first DATA again, or from line N
A$ + B$             LEN ASC VAL INSTR CHR$ STR$ HEX$ LEFT$ MID$ RIGHT$ TIME$
ON X GOTO N, M, ... go on at the line of the X-th number, or GOSUB it
DEF FNA(X) = E      the function FNA of X, as FNA(2); FNA$ gives a string
DO [WHILE X]        the statements up to LOOP while X, or DO UNTIL X
LOOP [UNTIL X]      back to DO, until X, or LOOP WHILE X
EXIT                on after the LOOP of the latest DO
IF X THEN           the lines up to ELSE or ENDIF when X, with THEN last
ELSE ... ENDIF      the lines up to ENDIF when not, each first in its line
DEF PROCA(X)        the lines up to ENDPROC, run by PROCA(2)
DEF FNA(X)          the lines up to = E, whose E is the value of FNA(2)
LOCAL X, Y$         X 0 and Y$ empty in a PROC or FN, until it ends
SLEEP S             wait S seconds, such as 0.5; TI is the microseconds counted
FOREGROUND N        the color of the characters, 0 to 15, or of R, G, B 0 to 15
BACKGROUND N        their background, likewise; 0 black, 1 white, 6 blue
CURSOR ON | OFF     show the cursor, or hide it
CURSOR C, R [, S]   the cursor to column C, row R; S 0 solid, 1 blinking
RCURSOR C, R        C and R the column and the row of the cursor
TRAP N              on an error, go on at line N; TRAP alone, stop at errors
RESUME [NEXT | N]   after TRAP: the statement again, the one after it, or line N
ER, EL, ERR$(N)     the last error's number and line, and the text of error N
DS, DS$             the last file or network failure: its number and text
CRSR keys, HOME     move the cursor; RETURN takes the line it is in
PgUp PgDn           scroll back; on the MEGA65 CTRL and MEGA with CRSR up, down
READY.
